# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole estate's
# secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ── THIS DEPLOYABLE IS THREE TITLES AND THREE DATABASES ──────────────────────
#
# Waves M3 and M4a of micro-deploy `docs/service-merge-plan.md`: emberkin
# absorbed aetherholm, then nda, so one image, one listener and one migrator now
# serve all three. The databases are NOT merged and must never be: FOUR table
# names exist in all three schemas WITH THE SAME COLUMNS (`outbox`,
# `event_subscriptions`, `outbox_deliveries`, `inbox`), and emberkin and
# aetherholm share `seasons` and `battles` on top of that. Pointing any two
# variables at one database is two `create table inbox` racing — and then one
# title's tables never created at all, with a migrator that exits 0.
# `src/migratortargets.ts` refuses that before it issues a statement.
#
# The four shared with the SAME columns are the sharp ones. A wrong handle
# against `seasons` is a 500, because the columns differ; a wrong handle against
# `inbox` is an INSERT that succeeds and dedupes an event that database has
# never seen.
#
# Rule 1 still holds. A SERVICE owns its databases and reads no OTHER service's;
# what changed is that one deployable is now three former services, which is why
# CI's `database-env-var` takes all three names.
EMBERKIN_DATABASE_URL=postgres://emberkin:emberkin@127.0.0.1:5432/emberkin
AETHERHOLM_DATABASE_URL=postgres://aetherholm:aetherholm@127.0.0.1:5432/aetherholm
NDA_DATABASE_URL=postgres://nda:nda@127.0.0.1:5432/nda

# ── SERVING BOTH NETWORKS FROM ONE POD ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both OPTIONAL and empty here: a development stack is one network. See
# micro-deploy `docs/network-consolidation.md`.
#
# Set the second DSN and this deployment holds a handle per network, choosing per
# request from the `CF-Network` header the gateway stamps. Empty, `networkSql`
# holds one handle and REFUSES a testnet request rather than answering it out of
# mainnet rows.
EMBERKIN_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
AETHERHOLM_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
NDA_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
#
# The network to assume when no `CF-Network` arrives. For `pnpm dev`, which has no
# gateway. NEVER in production: there an unstamped request is a routing fault, and
# guessing turns it into a silent cross-network write.
CF_NETWORK_SINGLE=


# Identity's JWKS and issuer. Verification is no longer the only thing this service asks identity
# for: since micro-org #228 it also POSTs to `/service-tokens/exchange` there. That exchange is
# dialled at IDENTITY_ISSUER unless IDENTITY_URL below overrides it, so a correct deployment needs
# no new variable to gain the refresh.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4001

# HMAC key for event signatures — outbound AND INBOUND.
#
# This is the key that verifies inbound events on POST /v1/events (entitlement grants from billing,
# account deletions from identity). An event webhook with no MAC lets anyone who can reach the port
# assert that a customer bought a season pass and have the reward granted. At least 32 random
# characters:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Leaving a placeholder here (CHANGE_ME) is refused at boot, deliberately. Do not paste one back in.
#
# The SCHEME is `@cloudsforge/contracts-events`': `cf-signature: t=<seconds>,v1=<hmac over
# "<seconds>.<body>">`. It used to be a local `x-cloudsforge-signature: sha256=<hmac over body>`,
# which no producer in the estate sends — so every inbound delivery answered 403 and every event
# this service emitted was refused by its consumers. SIGNING is always exactly one key.
# GENERATE IT, DO NOT TYPE IT. Since micro-org #212 this is held to the shape a generator produces
# — base64 or hex and nothing else, at least 32 decoded BYTES, and a measured entropy floor — so
# `CHANGE_ME`, which used to sit here, is refused at boot. Run: openssl rand -base64 48
OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET=

# The upstreams. Billing answers what an account owns (cosmetics, season passes); the ledger records
# a cosmetic purchase's posting; worlds owns the shared player profile and achievements.
LEDGER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4007
BILLING_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4009
WORLDS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000

# This service's own LONG-LIVED, REVOCABLE service credential — `cfsc_…`, never a JWT.
#
# THIS IS WHAT A CORRECT DEPLOYMENT SETS, and `EMBERKIN_SERVICE_TOKEN` below is the thing it
# replaces. `src/upstreams.ts` exchanges this at `POST /service-tokens/exchange` for an ordinary
# short-lived token and re-mints it before it expires, driven by the `expiresIn` the exchange itself
# answered with. The credential does not expire and the exchange consumes nothing, so every replica
# boots from the same value and a restart weeks later still works.
#
# NOT shared with any other service — SD-05. It must carry: billing:read, ledger:post, worlds:write
# (to post achievements to the profile).
#
# A TOKEN PASTED HERE IS REFUSED AT BOOT, by name, and that refusal is the point rather than
# pedantry: a ten-minute JWT in this slot would exchange nothing, work for ten minutes and fail
# exactly the way micro-org #228 describes. Mint one with: deploy/scripts/estate-bootstrap.sh
EMBERKIN_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL=

# ── AND THE ABSORBED nda MODULE'S OWN CREDENTIAL (wave M4a) ───────────────────
#
# A SECOND credential in one pod, and that is deliberate rather than an
# oversight. Each module exchanges as ITSELF: worlds and billing authorise
# `service:nda` and `service:emberkin` differently, and one shared credential
# would give either title the other's authority for the sake of one fewer
# variable. `src/nda/env.ts` is the only file that reads this one.
#
# UNSET IS A HARD READINESS FAILURE FOR THE WHOLE POD, and that is worth knowing
# before a deploy rather than after: the nda module contributes a hard
# `nda-identity-credential` probe, so without this `/readyz` answers 503 — while
# `/livez` stays 200 — and `aetherholm-web` renders that as AETHERHOLM being
# down, because emberkin publishes no readiness router of its own.
#
# Mint one with: deploy/scripts/estate-bootstrap.sh
NDA_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL=

# The old scoped service TOKEN — a ten-minute JWT. **A MIGRATION AID WITH A STATED END. LEAVE THIS
# UNSET.**
#
# THIS VARIABLE IS micro-org #228. The composition root read it once, at import, and presented it
# verbatim as a Bearer to billing, the ledger and worlds for the life of the process — so a replica
# authenticated for the first ten minutes it was alive and presented a dead token afterwards, with
# /livez green throughout because /livez verifies nothing. A longer expiry is not the fix; the token
# expiring IS the rotation, and lengthening it moves the same failure later and makes it worse.
#
# It is still accepted so that a container carrying the old variable and not yet the new one keeps
# BOOTING through a rolling deploy rather than exiting 1 mid-flight. While it is in use the service
# logs `fatal` at boot naming what will break, and `emberkin_service_token_static` reads 1.
#
# EXACTLY ONE OF THE TWO IS REQUIRED. Setting neither is refused at boot, which pins the deploy
# ordering: add the credential before removing the token, or the container says so at the door
# instead of ten minutes later on a player's purchase.
#
# When the value IS set its shape is checked exactly as before (micro-org #212), so `CHANGE_ME` and
# the compose fallback `estate-placeholder-token-0000000000000000` are still refused.
# EMBERKIN_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

# The keys POST /v1/events will ACCEPT, comma-separated, NEWEST FIRST. Unset, it is exactly
# [OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET] — today's behaviour byte for byte, so setting nothing changes nothing.
#
# A LIST rather than a value because the estate's outbox key is ONE secret shared by 24 services,
# and one key cannot be rotated by swapping it: whichever end moves first has every delivery between
# them refused until the other catches up, and the failure does not announce itself — delivery
# partitions, and the symptom reads as a secret mismatch rather than as a deploy ordering problem.
# So the rotation is a window. Add the new key at the FRONT, restart, move the producers, then drop
# the old one:
#   OUTBOX_ACCEPT_SECRETS=<the new key>,<the old key>
# Every entry faces the same bar as the signing secret — at least 24 characters, no placeholder —
# and listing the same key twice is refused, because a duplicate makes "which key verified this"
# ambiguous and that answer is how you know the rotation is finished.
# OUTBOX_ACCEPT_SECRETS=

# Identity's ORIGIN, where the credential above is exchanged. Defaults to IDENTITY_ISSUER, which is
# identity's origin on both estates — this exists for the day the issuer becomes a public URL and
# the in-cluster address stops matching it. An ORIGIN, so a path is refused at boot rather than
# producing a `POST /v1//service-tokens/exchange` that 404s with nothing naming the cause.
# IDENTITY_URL=

PORT=4100
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
EMBERKIN_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10
# The absorbed module's own pool, sized independently: the two titles have very
# different query shapes and one budget for both would make a fleet sweep and a
# battle submission contend for the same connections.
AETHERHOLM_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10
NDA_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# EMBERKIN_UPSTREAM_DEADLINE_MS=5000

# The absorbed nda module's three tunables. Optional, and the defaults are the
# ones micro-nda shipped: `NDA_TICK_LEASE_MS` is how long a day resolution may
# hold its lease before another worker may claim it, `NDA_TICK_BATCH_SIZE` how
# many due worlds one sweep fans out.
# NDA_UPSTREAM_DEADLINE_MS=5000
# NDA_TICK_LEASE_MS=120000
# NDA_TICK_BATCH_SIZE=50

# The absorbed nda module's legacy service TOKEN. **LEAVE THIS UNSET**, exactly
# as EMBERKIN_SERVICE_TOKEN above: it is DETECTED and IGNORED, and setting it
# logs an error naming the credential that replaces it.
# NDA_SERVICE_TOKEN=

# The reward budget a new season is opened with when nobody names one, in **EMBER wei** — the asset
# this service grants in since micro-org#226. It was denominated in SHARD, which the estate retired
# on 2026-08-04 and which `assertIssuable` refuses by name.
#
# **A MONEY CONTROL, NOT A TUNING KNOB.** Season rewards are ledger postings, so a game exploit that
# mints rewards is a money incident. The cap is enforced by the database in the same transaction as
# the posting, so no application-level cleverness can spend past it. Deliberately small: a budget
# nobody chose should bind long before it costs anything.
#
# The default below is 4,000 EMBER — the 100,000 Shards this used to default to, CONVERTED at the
# rate migration 9 freezes (100 Shards to the USD, 0.25 USD to the EMBER, so 1 Shard = 4e16 wei) and
# not relabelled. Eighteen decimals means the figure is past Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER, so it is read
# as a bigint and a decimal string is the only accepted form.
#
# **EMBERKIN_SEASON_REWARD_BUDGET_SHARDS IS REFUSED AT BOOT**, not accepted-and-ignored. A
# deployment still setting it is asserting a budget in a unit this build no longer has, and falling
# back to the default would be a budget nobody chose presented as one somebody did — wrong by
# eighteen orders of magnitude, in the direction of generosity. (An earlier version of this comment
# justified keeping the old name by saying it "is set on the estate". It is not: measured
# 2026-08-10 against the running container and across deploy/compose, neither name appears
# anywhere, so mainnet has always run on the default.)
# EMBERKIN_SEASON_REWARD_BUDGET_WEI=4000000000000000000000

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by and in a lease's owner column. Defaults to the hostname,
# which is the container id under compose and the pod name under Kubernetes.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line and in the release manifest.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded ahead
# of the process, not by src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here.
#
# EMBERKIN_TEST_DATABASE_URL is read only by the test suite, and only when its name contains "test"
# — the database-backed tests truncate.
